        "problem": "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n}=$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={({e}^{y})}^{-4} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
